fix(sync): carry kit definitions in sync and portable backups (#151)

`kit_components` was left out of `SYNC_TABLES` in the v1 baseline as
"device-local". That list drives both the sync snapshot and the portable
`backup.json`, so kits never reached a user's other devices and a restore
from a backup taken without the raw-SQLite option silently dropped every
kit definition.

Kit edges already carry `id`, `updated_at` and the auto-stamp trigger, so
they join the set as ordinary last-write-wins rows, ordered after `items`
and registered in `FK_REFS` (both endpoints are NOT NULL / ON DELETE
CASCADE, so an edge whose kit or component did not survive a merge is
dropped rather than tripping a foreign key). Removing a component now
writes its tombstone in the same transaction as the DELETE, so the removal
propagates instead of being re-downloaded from a peer.

Unlike `item_relations`, a kit edge's id is a random UUID rather than
derived from its endpoints, so two devices adding the same component to
the same kit invent two ids for one `UNIQUE (kit_item_id, component_item_id)`
pair. That collision is settled by the natural-key pass, which also gained
a general fix the kit case exposed: when a peer has *already* retired the
loser, its tombstone arrives alongside the winner and the merge stopped
detecting a collision at all — yet the doomed row was still holding the
key when the winner's INSERT ran, because tombstone DELETEs are applied
after the UPSERTs. Those are now recorded as hoist-only resolutions that
pull the DELETE forward without retiring anything, so the abort is avoided
for every table with a non-primary-key UNIQUE index, not just kits.

